      <!--Interaction End -->


      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00036">If you use your own name as the input to G, explain how many outputs are possible and how you know.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">


          <para id="para-00037">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00038">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. One possible output. I can only graduate from high school once. </para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->


      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00039">If you use your graduation date as the input to P, explain how many outputs are possible and how you know.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">


          <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer:</para>

          <para id="para-00041">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Many possible outputs. There are other people who will share the same graduation date as mine - especially my classmates who graduate with me. </para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->


      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00042">Only one of these two relationships, G or P, is a function. The other is not a function. Which one is the function?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">


          <para id="para-00043">Compare your answers:</para>

          <para id="para-00044">Rule G is a function and Rule P is not.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->


      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00045">How do you know the relationship you chose is a function?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">


          <para id="para-00046">Compare your answers:</para>

          <para id="para-00047">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. G only assigns one output to the input value.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->


      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>For the relationship that is a function, write two input-output pairs from the table using function notation.</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00011">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00048">Enter your first input-output pair example here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00011">


          <para id="para-00049">Compare your answers:</para>

          <para id="para-00050">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples. \(G(Taylor Swift)\) = 2008 \(G(Storm Reid)\) = 2020 </para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->

      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00012">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00051">Enter your second input-output pair example here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00012">


          <para id="para-00052">Compare your answers:</para>

          <para id="para-00053">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples. \(G(Zendaya)\) = 2015 \(G(Johnny Orlando)\) = 2021 </para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->


      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00013">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>

            <para id="para-00054"> Write a rule that describes these input-output pairs:\(F\)(ONE) \(=3\)\(F\)(TWO) \(=3\)\(F\)(THREE) \(=5\)\(F\)(FOUR) \(=4\) What rule could define function \(F\)? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">


            <para id="para-00055">Compare your answers:</para>

            <para id="para-00056">Function \(F\) takes a word for its input and gives the number of letters in that word for its output.</para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->

        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00014">
          <problem id="prob-00014">
            <para id="para-00057"> Here are some input-output pairs with the same inputs but different outputs:\(v\)(ONE) \(=2\) \(v\)(TWO) \(=1\) \(v\)(THREE) \(=2\) \(v\)(FOUR) \(=2\) What rule could define function \(v\)? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00014">


            <para id="para-00058">Compare your answers:</para>

            <para id="para-00059">Function \(v\) takes a word for its input and gives the number of vowels in that word as its output.</para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001"><title>Self Check</title>
  <para id="para-00060">The price for pizzas, in dollars, is a function of the number of pizzas bought, \(n\). Identify the input, output, and meaning of the statement: \(p(11)=55\)</para></note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Meanings in Functions </title>
        <para id="para-00061">Review the following examples to help you gain a better understanding of meanings in functions.</para>
        <exercise id="exer-00015">
          <problem id="prob-00015">

            <para id="para-00062"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>

            <para id="para-00063">The number of cubic yards of dirt, \(D\), needed to cover a garden with an area of \(a\) square feet is given by \(D=g(a)\). </para>
            <para id="para-00064">A garden with area 5000 \(ft^2\) requires 50 \(yd^3\) of dirt. Express this information in terms of the function \(g\).</para>
            <para id="para-00065">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00066">
              <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> \(g(5000)=50\) </para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="exer-00016">
          <problem id="prob-00016">

            <para id="para-00067">Explain the meaning of the statement \(g(100)=1\).</para>
            <para id="para-00068">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00016">
            <para id="para-00069">
              <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> The number of cubic yards of dirt required for a garden of 100 square feet is 1. </para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="exer-00017">
          <problem id="prob-00017">

            <para id="para-00070"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>

            <para id="para-00071">Let \(f(t)\) be the number of ducks in a lake \(t\) years after 1990. Explain the meaning of each statement:</para>

            <para id="para-00072">\(f(5)=30\) </para>
            <para id="para-00073">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00017">
            <para id="para-00074">
              <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> In 1995 (5 years after 1990), there were 30 ducks in the lake. </para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="exer-00018">
          <problem id="prob-00018">

            <para id="para-00075">\(f(10)=40\)</para>
            <para id="para-00076">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00018">
            <para id="para-00077">
              <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> In 2000 (10 years after 1990), there were 40 ducks in the lake. </para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Meanings in Functions</title>
    <para id="para-00078">The amount of garbage, \(G\), produced by a city with population \(p\) is given by \(G=f(p)\). \(G\) is measured in tons per week, and \(p\) is measured in thousands of people.</para>
    <exercise id="exer-00019">
            <problem id="prob-00019">
              <para id="para-00079">The town of Tola has a population of 40,000 and produces 13 tons of garbage each week. Express this information in terms of the function \(f\).</para>
              <para id="para-00080">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00019">


              <para id="para-00081">Compare your answer:</para>

              <para id="para-00082">\(13=f(40)\)</para>

            </solution>
          </exercise>
    <exercise id="exer-00020">
            <problem id="prob-00020">
              <para id="para-00083">Explain the meaning of the statement \(f(5)=2\).</para>
              <para id="para-00084">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00020">


              <para id="para-00085">Compare your answer:</para>

              <para id="para-00086">Five thousand people will produce 2 tons of garbage each week.</para>

            </solution>
          </exercise></note>
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
